Evaluate task evidence without treating a model as observed usability (@computing-trust-evaluation: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Investigate-intent-permissions-uncertain-outcomes-and-minimal-receipts.lingua#r_006DE209B98FF46901A89BC981") { r_00702DC8214B4953486BC85FE3
Three different activities answer different questions. A mathematical argument checks implications of explicit premises. Executing a finite model checks its enumerated behavior. Observing people attempting representative tasks provides evidence about their interaction in that setting. None substitutes for the others. Reuse [Connect assessment tasks to the claims their responses can support](../../inquiry/core/Connect-assessment-tasks-to-the-claims-their-responses-can-support.lingua#r_002B495CD7777FBC8C28058BF0), [Compare learning arrangements without confusing practice with evidence of an effect](../../inquiry/core/Compare-learning-arrangements-without-confusing-practice-with-evidence-of-an-effect.lingua#r_0045A77E6ECD510BD893851188) and [Review causal conclusions with their design assumptions and remaining ambiguity](../../inquiry/core/Review-causal-conclusions-with-their-design-assumptions-and-remaining-ambiguity.lingua#r_0075B44A727D0D560E9EDE6798).

For the ledger task, distinguish correct operation from correct interpretation. A participant can produce the intended total yet believe a timeout means cancellation; that misunderstanding matters when the next attempt is different. Conversely, a participant can correctly report an unknown outcome although the server has already committed. Their report is appropriate to the evidence they can access. Scoring it against hidden server state alone would penalize sound reasoning.

Design three cases before any session: a normal confirmation, a dropped request and a lost reply after commit. The last two must initially look the same to the participant. Ask what happened, what is known, what remains uncertain and what action they would take. A rule that always reports success fails the dropped-request case. A rule that always reports failure after timeout fails the committed case. A rule that preserves uncertainty and retries the same operation is consistent with both, assuming the service retains its receipt and permission allows recovery. Changing the request identity creates a useful failed rival.

A proposed evaluation should describe participant roles and relevant access needs, task wording, devices and assistive technology, prior knowledge, assistance, interruptions, consent and data handling, stopping conditions and scoring. Record observable completions, wrong-object effects, repeated additions, interpretation errors, recovery attempts and requests for help. Do not manufacture timings or attribute a failure to the person without investigating confusing wording, missing feedback or unsupported input. A small formative observation can expose a defect without estimating its population frequency.

If comparing two interfaces, define the outcome and assignment process. Practice, order, familiarity and unequal assistance can change performance independently of the design. Counterbalancing or random assignment helps only with the problem it actually addresses; use the inquiry lessons to delimit the causal claim. Report who and what was tested, not an unqualified statement that everyone can use it.

**Work:** prepare a task sheet, observation sheet and interpretation rubric with separate columns for effect, evidence, understanding and assistance. Predict contrasting responses from the three stipulated rules above. Revise one ambiguous question. A later learner may conduct an appropriate small evaluation and report actual findings; this authored block contains scenarios and model evidence only, with no participants, observations or accessibility certification claimed.

**Continue:** [Investigate intent permissions uncertain outcomes and minimal receipts](Investigate-intent-permissions-uncertain-outcomes-and-minimal-receipts.lingua#r_006DE209B98FF46901A89BC981).

